{{also|Balsam}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l/en|balsem}}, {{l/en|balsum}}, {{l/en|balsome}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} *{{term||balsam|lang=enm}}, {{term|balsme|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{term|balsam|lang=ang}}, {{term|balsamum||balsam, balm|lang=ang}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{term|balsamum|lang=la}}, from {{etyl|grc|en}} {{term|βάλσαμον||balsam|lang=grc}}, of {{etyl|sem|en}} origin.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|bôlʹsəm}}, {{IPA|/ˈbɔːlsəm/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{cx|chiefly|UK|lang=en}} A sweet-smelling [[oil]] or [[resin]] derived from various plants.
# {{cx|chiefly|UK|lang=en}} A plant or tree yielding such substance.
# {{cx|chiefly|UK|lang=en}} A [[soothing]] [[ointment]].
# {{cx|chiefly|UK|figuratively|lang=en}} Something soothing.
#: ''Classical music is a sweet '''balsam''' for our sorrows
# A [[flower]]ing plant of the genus ''[[Impatiens]]''.
# A [[balsam fir]].
# [[Canada balsam]], a turpentine obtained from the resin of balsam fir.

